# Publish to a private index

For releasing to your organisation's package index (Artifactory, Nexus, devpi, a cloud artifact
registry, GitLab's package registry) instead of, or before, PyPI. Any index that implements the
[simple repository API](https://packaging.python.org/en/latest/specifications/simple-repository-api/)
for downloads and the legacy upload API for uploads works.

## 1. Point the release pipeline at the index

Set three variables as masked {% if git_hosting_service == 'github' %}secrets on the `pypi` environment (*Settings → Environments → pypi*){% else %}CI/CD variables scoped to the `pypi` environment (*Settings → CI/CD → Variables*){% endif %}:

| Variable | Value |
| --- | --- |
| `UV_PUBLISH_URL` | The index's **upload** URL, e.g. `https://pkgs.example.com/api/pypi/internal/` |
| `UV_PUBLISH_USERNAME` | The account or token name the index gives you |
| `UV_PUBLISH_PASSWORD` | The password or token |

Optionally add `UV_PUBLISH_CHECK_URL` with the index's **simple** URL (the one you would install
from, e.g. `https://pkgs.example.com/api/pypi/internal/simple/`). uv then skips files the index
already has, so re-running a publish that failed halfway does not fail on the half that
succeeded.

The environment name stays `pypi` even when the index is not PyPI: it is the name the publish
job declares, and renaming it means renaming it there too.

## 2. Release as usual

`release.publish` sees all three `UV_PUBLISH_*` variables set and runs:

```bash
uv publish --trusted-publishing never --no-attestations
```

`--no-attestations` because PEP 740 attestations are accepted only by PyPI through Trusted
Publishing, and an index that does not support them may reject the whole upload. For the same
reason `release.attest` signs nothing on this path.

## 3. Install from it

To depend on packages from the private index in this project, declare it in `pyproject.toml`:

```toml
[[tool.uv.index]]
name = "internal"
url = "https://pkgs.example.com/api/pypi/internal/simple/"
publish-url = "https://pkgs.example.com/api/pypi/internal/"
explicit = true
```

`explicit = true` makes uv use this index only for packages you pin to it
(`[tool.uv.sources] some-package = { index = "internal" }`), so a name that exists on both
cannot be served from the wrong one. With `publish-url` set you can also publish by hand:

```bash
./workflow.cmd release.dist
uv publish --index internal --no-attestations
```

`--index` uses the index's `url` as the check URL automatically.

## Never a `.pypirc`

Twine-era guides keep credentials in `~/.pypirc` (the [.pypirc format](https://packaging.python.org/en/latest/specifications/pypirc/)).
uv does not read it, and the project gitignores `.pypirc` so one created in the tree by habit is
not committed. Use the variables above, or `uv auth login` for an interactive session.

